This repository contains personal dotfiles and configurations, managed
using Git and GNU Stow.

GNU Stow is a symlink farm manager which takes distinct packages of
software and/or data located in a single directory tree and makes them
appear to be installed in the same place.

--- Repository Structure -----------------------------------------------

The repository is structured to mirror the exact paths where the files
will reside in the home directory, grouped within their respective
packages.

~/dotfiles/
|-- README
|-- deploy.sh
|-- bash/
|   |-- .bash_aliases -> symlinked to ~/.bash_aliases
|   |-- .bash_profile -> symlinked to ~/.bash_profile
|   |-- .bashrc -> symlinked to ~/.bashrc
|   `-- .local/bin/
|       `-- ssh-sendmail -> symlinked to ~/.local/bin/ssh-sendmail
|-- firefox/
|   `-- .mozilla/
|       `-- firefox/
|           |-- profiles.ini -> symlinked to
|           |   ~/.mozilla/firefox/profiles.ini
|           |-- main.profile/
|               `-- user.js -> symlinked to
|                   ~/.mozilla/firefox/main.profile/user.js
|           `-- developer-edition.profile/
|               `-- user.js -> symlinked to
|                   ~/.mozilla/firefox/developer-edition.profile/user.js
|-- firefox-policy/  # internal support for the firefox package
|   `-- etc/
|       `-- firefox/
|           `-- policies/
|               `-- policies.json -> installed by ./deploy.sh firefox to
|                   /etc/firefox/policies/policies.json
|-- git/
|   `-- .gitconfig -> symlinked to ~/.gitconfig
|-- pipewire/
|   `-- .config/
|       `-- pipewire/
|           `-- pipewire.conf.d/
|               `-- 50-hardcode-alsa.conf -> symlinked to
|                   ~/.config/pipewire/pipewire.conf.d/
|                   50-hardcode-alsa.conf
|-- vim/
|   `-- .vim/
|       `-- vimrc -> symlinked to ~/.vim/vimrc
`-- x11/
    `-- .xinitrc -> symlinked to ~/.xinitrc

--- Deployment ---------------------------------------------------------

To deploy these dotfiles on a fresh Arch Linux installation:

1. Clone the repository into ~/dotfiles:
   $ git clone https://github.com/librefos/dotfiles.git ~/dotfiles
   $ cd ~/dotfiles

2. Execute the interactive deployment script:
   $ ./deploy.sh

The script will automatically detect available packages and prompt you
to provide the names of the packages to deploy, comma-separated.  You
can also automate execution by passing the names directly as an
argument.

The `firefox` package also installs Firefox enterprise policies into
`/etc/firefox/policies` with `sudo`, because Firefox does not read this
policy type from the home directory.  This keeps browser setup as one
command:

   $ ./deploy.sh firefox

The `developer-edition.profile` profile is the main daily profile for
Firefox Developer Edition.  It sets a compact interface, dark theme,
blank startup/new-tab pages, downloads in `/tmp`, the XDG portal file
picker, Lite DuckDuckGo search, reduced Firefox Suggest surfaces, and
developer tools with persistent logs, measurement, screenshot,
responsive-design buttons, and a right-docked toolbox.

Both profiles use the Default theme with the Dark appearance and "Use
Linux system theme" checked, so the toolbars take the GTK theme's dark
colours; there is no `userChrome.css`.  Developer Edition's own default
is the Dark theme, which `user.js` only overrides in a new profile, so an
existing one is switched once by hand in about:addons > Themes.  The
sidebar's launcher column of tool icons is hidden; the sidebar button in
the nav bar still opens it.

The policies in `/etc/firefox/policies/policies.json` complement the
profile with global defaults.  Camera, microphone, location,
notifications, virtual reality, and local-network access start blocked by
default, but site permissions are not locked; adjust them inside Firefox
when you need calls or a specific site exception.  AI controls remain
blocked by policy.  uBlock Origin is installed from addons.mozilla.org
on the next start of Firefox, also in private windows, and keeps
updating from there; it can be disabled but not removed.

To get a parseable list of available packages for use in scripts, pass
the `--list` flag.

If it detects existing regular files at the target locations (such as
a default ~/.bashrc), it will safely rename them with a .bak extension
before creating the symlinks.

If you need to make machine-specific adjustments to these configs,
prefer putting them in separate local files (e.g., ~/.bash_local) and
sourcing them from the main configuration, rather than modifying the
tracked files in this repository.
